# Frontend Rules for Taskify

Use the rules below when:

* You are writing **HTML**
* You are writing **CSS**
* You are writing styles in a **JavaScript/Stimulus controller**

---

## Design System Overview

Taskify uses a **custom CSS design system** with CSS variables for theming and a custom icon system. The design is defined in:

```
app/assets/stylesheets/application.css
```

---

### ✅ Always start here

* **Before** you write any markup or styles, reference `application.css` to understand:

  * **CSS custom properties** (CSS variables) for colors and themes
  * **Custom icon system** using CSS pseudo-elements
  * **Animation keyframes** for transitions and interactions
  * **Component styles** for cards, buttons, forms, etc.

---

### ✅ Always prefer existing CSS variables

Use the CSS custom properties defined in `:root` and `.dark-mode` whenever possible.

Examples:

* ✅ **Good**

  * `color: var(--text-primary)` (instead of hardcoded colors)
  * `background-color: var(--bg-primary)` (instead of `background-color: white`)
  * `border: 1px solid var(--border-color)` (instead of hardcoded border colors)
* ❌ **Avoid**

  * Hardcoded hex colors unless absolutely necessary
  * Re-creating color schemes that already exist

### Available CSS Variables

**Light Mode Colors:**
```css
--bg-primary: #F9F8F7;
--bg-secondary: #EDEAE4;
--bg-tertiary: #ADB8BB;
--text-primary: #232A2F;
--text-secondary: #153147;
--text-muted: #ADB8BB;
--accent-primary: #153147;
--success: #153147;
```

**Dark Mode:** Automatically applied via `.dark-mode` class

---

### ✅ Use the custom icon system

* Icons are created using CSS pseudo-elements with the `.icon` class
* **Always** use the existing icon classes like `.icon-plus`, `.icon-edit`, `.icon-delete`
* Icons are defined in `application.css` using Unicode characters in `::before` pseudo-elements

Examples:
```html
<!-- Good -->
<span class="icon icon-plus"></span>
<button class="btn"><span class="icon icon-edit"></span> Edit</button>

<!-- Available icons -->
.icon-plus, .icon-edit, .icon-delete, .icon-check, .icon-clock,
.icon-calendar, .icon-warning, .icon-stats, .icon-sun, .icon-moon,
.icon-clipboard, .icon-tasks, .icon-user, .icon-bell, .icon-search
```

---

### ✅ Follow existing component patterns

* Use existing CSS classes for common components:
  * `.btn`, `.btn-primary`, `.btn-secondary` for buttons
  * `.card`, `.card-header`, `.card-body` for cards
  * `.form-group`, `.form-label`, `.form-input` for forms
  * `.status-badge`, `.priority-badge` for badges
  * `.notification-*` classes for notifications

---

### ✅ Leverage CSS animations

* Use the predefined `@keyframes` animations:
  * `fadeIn`, `slideIn`, `scaleIn` for entrance animations
  * `pulse`, `bounce`, `float` for interactive feedback
  * `fadeUp`, `slideInLeft` for page transitions

Example:
```css
.new-element {
  animation: fadeIn 0.3s ease-out;
}
```

---

### 🚫 Never create new styles arbitrarily

* **Do not** add new CSS variables or major style changes without understanding the existing system
* **Do not** create new icon fonts or icon systems - use the existing CSS pseudo-element approach
* If you believe a new style is needed:

  * **First**, check if existing classes can be combined or modified
  * Follow the existing naming patterns and color system

---

### ✅ Always generate semantic HTML

* Use the correct HTML elements (`button`, `nav`, `section`, etc.) rather than relying on `<div>` and `<span>` for everything
* Make sure your markup is accessible and follows the existing patterns in the views
* Follow the existing form patterns and card layouts

---

### ✅ Theme Support

* All styles support both light and dark modes automatically
* Dark mode is toggled via the `.dark-mode` class on the HTML element
* Use CSS variables to ensure your styles work in both themes
* Test your styles in both light and dark modes

---

✅ **Reminder:** Consistency with the existing design system is more important than personal style preferences.
✅ **Tip:** When in doubt, review existing components in the views before creating new markup or styles.

